```
ROLE: theory_skeptic  VOTE: red
MUNDANE_EXPLANATIONS:
  - The "core finding" p* = P/(S+P) with S = min((1-lead)*t_args, t_tool) - c_commit and P = w*t_tool is the textbook speculative-execution break-even (Smith 1981 branch prediction; Hennessy & Patterson Ch. 3 speculation cost model; LLM speculative decoding break-even in Leviathan et al. 2023 arXiv:2211.17192 and Chen et al. 2023 arXiv:2302.01318 has IDENTICAL form: gain capped by overlapped work, penalty linear in wasted compute). The "p* -> 1 as r grows" asymmetry is not emergent — it is the immediate algebraic consequence of min(a,b) saturating while a linear term does not. No new phenomenon; this is Amdahl's law applied to a speculative critical-section, restated.
  - The "agent-step framing" delta the orchestrator floats is cosmetic: substituting (decode, tool-call) for (decode, draft-verify) or (branch-resolve, squash-pipeline) does not change the math. The model has no agent-specific term.
  - "lead matters adversely" is not a finding — it falls out of S shrinking with (1-lead) while P is lead-independent. Pre-registration even predicts this. Restating a tautology as a result.
CONFOUNDS:
  - t_name is asserted to "cancel out" but appears in BOTH arms identically only under the assumption that name-decode cannot itself be overlapped with anything upstream (prior-step tool result handoff, KV-cache prefill of next step). In real agent loops it can — making baseline measured-latency lower than modeled and shrinking the win region further. Not tested.
  - w in [0,1] excludes side-effecting tools where rollback cost makes w>1 (compensating transactions, external API undo, user-visible state). The pre-reg acknowledges this in prose but the sweep does NOT include w>1, so the negative is actually UNDERSTATED for the realistic agent-tool mix (writes, posts, sends).
  - c_commit = 0.05*t_args is asserted, not derived. If commit requires re-running a validator/parser over full args (likely for JSON-schema-validated tool calls), c_commit scales with t_args and could be materially larger; sensitivity not swept.
  - "Tool runs CONCURRENTLY with remaining decode" assumes zero contention. On a real serving stack the speculative tool call can contend for the same GPU (if tool is another model call), network egress queue, or rate-limit token bucket as the ongoing decode — coupling t_tool and t_args. Not modeled.
  - p_correct is treated as a scalar, but in real traces it is conditional on tool identity, leading-arg fraction, prompt distribution, and prior turns. Marginalizing over a single p hides the realistic case where the few tools with high p_correct are exactly the cheap-and-fast ones (r<1) where the win is already small.
ASSUMPTIONS_UNTESTED:
  - Independence of p_correct from r: the model assumes correctness rate is exogenous, but tools with large t_tool (RAG, search, code execution) typically have MORE variable arguments (longer arg JSON, more free-text fields), so empirically p_correct likely DECREASES as r increases — compounding the negative. Untested and not in sensitivity sweep.
  - Lognormal multiplicative jitter σ=0.15 is asserted with no calibration to any measured decode/tool-latency distribution. Real tool latencies are heavy-tailed (network, retries) — under heavy tails the expected-value break-even understates tail-risk, and a risk-averse scheduler would set a higher effective p*.
  - "Fully serial redo" worst case vs "optimistic partial overlap" variant — the optimistic variant is mentioned but the headline tables are w=1 fully-serial; the asymmetric reporting biases the reader toward the negative. (Honest, but note that the negative is partly a reporting choice.)
  - No model of BATCH effects: in a served agent, speculative tool fires inflate the in-flight tool queue and can degrade tail latency for OTHER concurrent agents — a system-level cost the single-step model cannot see. Negative likely stronger at system level; positive cannot get stronger.
STATISTICAL_GAPS:
  - 5 seeds × 4000 trials gives tight MC error (max |analytic-MC|=0.049 at σ=0.15 is consistent), but seeds vary only the jitter — they do NOT vary p_correct (treated as a parameter, not sampled). No bootstrap CI on the break-even p* itself; the p* table is reported as point estimates.
  - No sensitivity analysis on c_commit, σ, or the optimistic-overlap variant — the realistic-regime win-fraction (51.8%) is a single point with no uncertainty band.
  - No statistical test that the analytic model and MC agree beyond a max-abs-diff number; e.g., no KS / no per-config residual distribution. "Max 0.049" could hide systematic bias at one corner.
  - Win-fraction (64.7% / 51.8%) is computed over a UNIFORM grid sweep — it is a property of the chosen grid, not of any realistic distribution over (r, lead, p, w). Reporting it as a headline is misleading; the only honest summary is the p* surface, which is given.
RATIONALE: The L0 result is mechanically correct but adds NO theoretical content beyond the classical speculative-execution break-even (Smith 1981; Hennessy & Patterson; LLM-spec-decoding Leviathan 2023 arXiv:2211.17192, Chen 2023 arXiv:2302.01318) — the saturation-vs-linear-penalty asymmetry is the textbook Amdahl/speculation ceiling, not a delta. The "agent-step framing" does not introduce any new term in the model; substituting names changes nothing. Worse, the model UNDERSTATES the negative by (a) excluding w>1 side-effecting tools, (b) assuming p_correct independent of r when empirically it likely anti-correlates, (c) ignoring resource contention coupling t_tool to t_args, (d) ignoring system-level batch effects, and (e) fixing c_commit without sensitivity. The genuine win region (r<1) is exactly where speculative decoding literature already says naive prefetch/speculation pays off least in absolute terms. A real-trace p_correct measurement WOULD be informative, but the THEORY pass cannot upgrade: the modeling delta is "we wrote down the standard equation for a new application" — that is a survey contribution, not a research claim. RED on the theory/modeling axis. The empirical p_correct measurement should be reframed as a measurement study, not a test of a novel mechanism.
```
